1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:03,200
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:03,860
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:08,660
My name is Catherine and today we have an intermediate level lesson about going to the bank.

4
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:09,360
That's right.

5
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:13,100
Today we're going to go to the bank and we need to do something there.

6
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:15,240
We're not going to put money in the bank actually.

7
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,480
We're going to make a transfer.

8
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:19,220
Okay, so you'll learn all about this in a minute.

9
00:00:19,720 --> 00:00:21,320
Let's take a listen to the dialogue first.

10
00:00:27,730 --> 00:00:28,430
Good morning.

11
00:00:28,950 --> 00:00:30,430
Welcome to Bank of the USA.

12
00:00:31,030 --> 00:00:31,950
How may I help you today?

13
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:34,690
Hi, I need to transfer some money to another account.

14
00:00:35,210 --> 00:00:35,430
It's urgent.

15
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:36,560
Okay.

16
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:38,940
Have you made a wire transfer at our bank before?

17
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:41,360
No, I've never made a transfer before.

18
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:43,500
It's all right.

19
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:44,360
I will take you through the procedure.

20
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:48,040
Are you transferring funds to a company or an individual account?

21
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:49,600
A company account.

22
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:50,800
I need to pay a bill.

23
00:00:51,580 --> 00:00:51,940
Okay.

24
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:58,220
I'll need the name of the company and their bank routing number as well as their bank's address and phone number.

25
00:00:58,860 --> 00:01:00,940
I have all the information in this folder.

26
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,220
Well, you've come prepared.

27
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:12,340
I have all the necessary materials so we can go ahead and make the transfer right now.

28
00:01:12,960 --> 00:01:15,680
It's a simple transaction and we can process it today.

29
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:18,060
Oh, that's such a relief.

30
00:01:18,740 --> 00:01:20,280
I didn't want the payment to be overdue.

31
00:01:20,660 --> 00:01:21,580
Thank you so much.

32
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:22,780
It's my pleasure.

33
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:27,620
All right, we're back.

34
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:30,260
So interesting dialogue, very common situation.

35
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:32,720
We need to transfer money to another account.

36
00:01:33,340 --> 00:01:35,600
So we have some very interesting vocab words right there.

37
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,040
Why don't we take a look at those on Language Takeaway.

38
00:01:40,050 --> 00:01:41,210
Language Takeaway.

39
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:46,510
So Marco, the first word is one that you've already said a couple of times in today's lesson.

40
00:01:46,730 --> 00:01:47,670
It is to transfer.

41
00:01:48,130 --> 00:01:48,930
Okay, that's right.

42
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:50,510
We need to transfer money.

43
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:53,210
So to transfer, what does this verb mean?

44
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:56,590
So to transfer means to move from one place to another.

45
00:01:56,850 --> 00:02:03,310
In the case of money, you're moving money from one bank to another bank or from one account to another account.

46
00:02:03,550 --> 00:02:05,470
So just think of transferring as moving.

47
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:06,460
That's right.

48
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,400
But the bank teller also mentioned something about a wire transfer.

49
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:12,380
So that's basically the same thing, right?

50
00:02:12,740 --> 00:02:13,140
That's right.

51
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:19,220
So when you're talking about money, you don't actually take a bag of money and you don't walk to another bank to put it in the account.

52
00:02:19,600 --> 00:02:22,760
A wire transfer means that the transfer is electronic.

53
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,820
One bank tells another bank that you're going to exchange this money.

54
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:31,000
So it's going to move from one person to another person or from one company to another company.

55
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,800
But it's never actually the money itself.

56
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,720
It's always just the number of funds in the account.

57
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:36,060
That's right.

58
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:41,440
Okay, so a wire transfer, to transfer money electronically from one account to another.

59
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:46,280
And talking about this word account, we're talking about a bank account.

60
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:47,280
What is this?

61
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:52,520
Well, this is really important even if you're not making a wire transfer or if you're not working at a bank.

62
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:56,200
This is something that most people have for their personal use.

63
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:01,720
So an account is a space for money in a bank that's kind of registered to one person or one company.

64
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:04,240
And there are many, many different kinds of accounts.

65
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:05,080
That's right.

66
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:07,540
Many of you probably have a savings account.

67
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:10,640
This is where you keep your money to save for the future.

68
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:21,460
Or many companies or people have a checking account or a default account where basically you put money in, but you're also taking money out and paying for bills and paying to go to the movies, etc.

69
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:23,800
Yeah, so these different accounts have different rules.

70
00:03:24,600 --> 00:03:29,020
They have rules about how much money you can keep in them or how much money you need to open them.

71
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:31,600
So the basic word here is account.

72
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:35,440
And just remember that companies and people can all have accounts at a bank.

73
00:03:35,440 --> 00:03:35,740
That's right.

74
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:40,400
Okay, so when talking about an account, we're talking about transferring money to another account.

75
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:42,740
We don't say we want to transfer money.

76
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:44,600
We say we want to transfer funds.

77
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,600
Funds.

78
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:50,640
So funds, we usually say in the plural, funds, with an S. This means money.

79
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:57,620
So we're talking about transferring funds from one company to another or I could say I need to deposit funds in my account.

80
00:03:58,040 --> 00:03:58,240
That's right.

81
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:06,900
Now an interesting word because funds with an S at the end, that's a plural noun as you said, but if we use it in the singular, it could also be a verb, to fund.

82
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:07,860
That's right.

83
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:10,300
And that means to pay for something or someone.

84
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:14,280
So my grandfather funded my college education.

85
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:17,320
He paid money for my college education.

86
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:17,510
That's right.

87
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:22,520
So both words, either the verb or the noun, have to do with money.

88
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:28,400
And when we're talking about transferring money, we're talking about a type of transaction, right?

89
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:32,800
Where a bank transfer or a wire transfer is a type of transaction.

90
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:33,800
That's right.

91
00:04:33,800 --> 00:04:41,940
So a transaction is a noun and it's something that you do at a bank, for example, usually involving the exchange of money.

92
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:50,800
So a wire transfer is a kind of transaction, but also depositing or putting money into my savings account is also a kind of transaction.

93
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:50,990
That's right.

94
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,710
So a transaction is kind of like a business move or a business deal involving money.

95
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:59,410
For example, a transaction can also be buying a car.

96
00:04:59,690 --> 00:04:59,990
That's right.

97
00:05:00,190 --> 00:05:07,230
So the transaction is I give my money to Marco, the car salesperson, and the transaction is exchanging that money for the car.

98
00:05:07,410 --> 00:05:07,910
That's right.

99
00:05:08,390 --> 00:05:08,750
Okay.

100
00:05:09,330 --> 00:05:17,010
And lastly, since we needed to pay a bill, we were worried that the bill will be overdue and that's why we really needed to make this wire transfer.

101
00:05:17,390 --> 00:05:17,690
Okay.

102
00:05:18,010 --> 00:05:20,690
So to be overdue is to be late.

103
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:22,320
So think about due.

104
00:05:22,320 --> 00:05:24,370
This is the time when something has to be done by.

105
00:05:25,240 --> 00:05:32,350
So for example, if the bill for the electricity is due on Wednesday, but I pay it on Thursday, it's overdue.

106
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:33,250
Okay.

107
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:37,630
So it not only has to do with money, it could also be something that is late.

108
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:44,570
For example, if I visit my grandma after not going there for three months, you would say this visit is long overdue.

109
00:05:44,970 --> 00:05:49,210
That means Marco should have visited his grandmother earlier, but he didn't.

110
00:05:49,330 --> 00:05:49,770
That's right.

111
00:05:50,230 --> 00:05:50,630
Okay.

112
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:56,900
So a lot of great vocab there all involving banks and different types of words you may find when transferring funds.

113
