147
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:27,080
So let's look at this word procedure.

148
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,360
Procedure can be many different things, but what is it in this case?

149
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:36,650
It's basically the steps or the process of taking money from the account and transferring it to another one.

150
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:37,550
That's the procedure.

151
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:44,080
So we have to do all the steps, you have to fill out the paperwork, you have to sign here and all these things.

152
00:07:44,080 --> 00:07:45,080
Right.

153
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:46,960
So we're talking about a wire transfer.

154
00:07:46,960 --> 00:07:49,610
So there is a procedure for doing a wire transfer.

155
00:07:49,770 --> 00:07:53,910
That means like you say, there are steps that we have to go through to complete this action.

156
00:07:54,470 --> 00:07:56,690
But we can also have a medical procedure.

157
00:07:57,200 --> 00:08:02,800
So a heart surgery has its own procedure, but that's very different from a wire transfer procedure.

158
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:03,800
That's right.

159
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:07,180
But it's still, it has different steps and different things that you must do in order to operate.

160
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:15,040
But when we have a bank teller who will take us through the procedure, this means that he or she will help us understand the steps we need to complete.

161
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:15,210
That's right.

162
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:22,070
So this, we could also use this in another bank scenario if maybe my little brother wants to open a bank account.

163
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:25,590
So I'm going to take him through the procedure of what he needs.

164
00:08:26,070 --> 00:08:28,990
He needs to maybe get a copy of his ID.

165
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:32,640
He needs to have $20, etc.

166
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:33,570
Take him through the procedure.

167
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:34,790
So you're going to help him.

168
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:36,040
All right.

169
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:37,040
That's right.

170
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:41,440
So this is something we say about why the person is so anxious to transfer this money.

171
00:08:41,440 --> 00:08:43,300
The person says, I have a company account.

172
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:45,220
I need to pay a bill.

173
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:50,680
So this is why the money is going to be transferred from one place to another to pay the bill.

174
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:55,160
So this is something we say not just about, you know, company things or business things.

175
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:58,000
We say about our things at home, like the electricity bill.

176
00:08:58,400 --> 00:08:58,620
That's right.

177
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:00,560
And actually this is more and more common.

178
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:05,090
We pay our bills using our electronic banking services.

179
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:11,560
So for example, maybe he needs to pay the electricity bill or the telephone bill and this is why we're transferring money.

180
00:09:11,560 --> 00:09:14,470
So to pay a bill is to cancel the bill.

181
00:09:14,930 --> 00:09:15,210
That's right.

182
00:09:15,390 --> 00:09:16,910
So I could say, it's time to pay the bills.

183
00:09:17,170 --> 00:09:17,890
It's the end of the month.

184
00:09:18,390 --> 00:09:20,790
Or, oh shoot, I forgot to pay the bills again.

185
00:09:21,130 --> 00:09:22,650
They're going to turn my electricity off.

186
00:09:22,790 --> 00:09:23,270
That's right.

187
00:09:23,710 --> 00:09:28,910
And now an interesting phrase here, very specific to making transfers.

188
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:32,950
When we talk about bank routing number, a bank routing number.

189
00:09:33,440 --> 00:09:34,550
Well this is pretty simple actually.

190
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,150
If you're going to transfer money, you need to be able to tell the bank where the money will go.

191
00:09:39,670 --> 00:09:41,110
So you can't just say Mr. Smith.

192
00:09:41,370 --> 00:09:44,230
You have to have a number for the account and each number is unique.

193
00:09:44,530 --> 00:09:47,270
It's special and it's usually very, very long.

194
00:09:47,850 --> 00:09:48,250
That's right.

195
00:09:49,080 --> 00:10:03,200
And actually it also has to do with what bank it is because obviously you can't just say, oh, Bank of USA because, you know, maybe there are two banks with the same name or maybe there's one Bank of USA in the United States and there's another one in, I don't know, in Europe.

196
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:04,200
Right.

197
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:07,320
So you have to have a certain number or letter combination in the bank routing number.

198
00:10:07,320 --> 00:10:16,000
And so the kind of account, is it savings or is it a business account and so on and so forth so that you know exactly where the money will go because you don't want to give the money to the wrong person.

199
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:16,620
That's right.

200
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:17,580
Okay.

